#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124180 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2f0fb4d-0acc-569d-889a-cccf1bebadd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322795} {:answer/id #uuid "8f366a3d-1d2f-506e-8ff1-34312d66b8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322796} {:answer/id #uuid "e91dcffa-a379-583e-8af0-c9b6ddd4d440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322797} {:answer/id #uuid "5584c912-1c0c-54a6-9fdf-5f244c2f54e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322798} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef773d5-ffa7-519e-8b79-fb00aab7d750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e91dcffa-a379-583e-8af0-c9b6ddd4d440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322797} :answer-field/id #uuid "93cba9eb-79c6-5b9c-a730-75a63e909d17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f1b31d1-29e5-5e40-8e1d-c102a9bf9bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130271" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edc8d069-2a81-5282-af66-4cea790ad699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802} {:answer/id #uuid "606f1354-4d09-58f4-9af2-39bcb9185301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322803} {:answer/id #uuid "db0b2d61-277d-5c7b-9c3a-fee9cce2666f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322804} {:answer/id #uuid "d053668f-a06a-57f6-b7e8-7e0ed8bbe6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805} {:answer/id #uuid "eab8bf65-89e9-5131-bf5c-cfbe31841a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606f1354-4d09-58f4-9af2-39bcb9185301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322803} :answer-field/id #uuid "643db0dd-1b50-5f94-a712-f9414f9879ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7c5aa5-5c3b-57a9-b613-006e91b71fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130724" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02751068-ad51-5264-a97f-ed1d0ab4ebf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322809} {:answer/id #uuid "bc802774-0b8b-589e-9209-b8912f2943d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322810} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ecbe-78d2-55ba-b3c7-550be513ff8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322811} {:answer/id #uuid "240eb88e-c808-5b31-86d3-255676d89869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322812} {:answer/id #uuid "00057f83-bb13-5625-8e40-bb4d96a6f0bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a598ecbe-78d2-55ba-b3c7-550be513ff8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322811} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb02f697-86e9-5804-abbd-791fbc7667f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a015312e-e549-5876-981e-21b6923273cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322816} {:answer/id #uuid "5eeab54b-0461-5b37-b48c-3b3ae249f7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322817} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc82a54-6770-583d-a42a-ec8448aa358b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322818} {:answer/id #uuid "de7d97b5-c531-5706-9be5-b9f9a1118fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322819} {:answer/id #uuid "56c21728-26d3-53c5-bae4-422f40392860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7d97b5-c531-5706-9be5-b9f9a1118fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322819} :answer-field/id #uuid "225ef13f-bb07-530a-98c0-5c877c04cd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aeaadd89-dc14-54c2-bff3-8d905f0aebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2a515a0-1ec1-5aba-92e6-0b0178195eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322823} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc8ef8e-31fe-52e1-b303-8f9fbc3cc657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322824} {:answer/id #uuid "fa458864-16e0-5032-88db-1a9e022fe159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322825} {:answer/id #uuid "e5764784-0edd-52ca-be9e-feeb012215a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322826} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaaae16-ece7-5a63-8545-bc4a5d1ea433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffaaae16-ece7-5a63-8545-bc4a5d1ea433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322827} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c49b1c-dab2-5442-af31-e47d008ee921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99fa8446-b5a9-5ac5-a425-7d1aaeb62335" :question/math-academy-id "q-131127" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c8ec18-b87f-5885-aa14-432139f277b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322830} {:answer/id #uuid "cc835021-dea2-5a65-82ec-0221f2a80e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322831} {:answer/id #uuid "d79dd1bf-8d44-5ec7-8ea6-f4d87908b642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322832} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7a2e5f-8044-58f8-bdca-b901aed23eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322833} {:answer/id #uuid "0059b6a0-a2d7-5fdb-b752-d8e015f173b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc835021-dea2-5a65-82ec-0221f2a80e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322831} :answer-field/id #uuid "549aa6f0-0020-509b-8885-63b47ef790e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c55cec54-efed-5c3b-a8a5-5b490ac1842f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131128" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}yz=-4$ at the point $(2,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ad2e308-0172-5965-bc1e-c9c18b0b865e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322837} {:answer/id #uuid "38bfb6ef-3482-5d6a-a1b0-e709ff632cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322838} {:answer/id #uuid "5333a7d8-85cc-5693-9601-3061dfb5c0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322839} {:answer/id #uuid "3460f4fc-6ff3-5068-b7ef-fc30163f8d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322840} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4fbd61-de16-537b-9bbc-326274088f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad2e308-0172-5965-bc1e-c9c18b0b865e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322837} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6c54c0-de64-5467-8fc5-cd3d04fffd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48fd28c2-a303-5fa6-8022-75364769fa65" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901739f6-6476-5b09-9d46-197bf5aaa672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122345} {:answer/id #uuid "f3107bf3-2907-5fee-b9d6-76c649c336b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122346} {:answer/id #uuid "218985c6-986f-5927-ac74-e7f1365b3d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + y + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122347} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4420b7-2d11-5d0b-af4b-bf52b0dda88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122348} {:answer/id #uuid "54c6ec15-8b9c-5bdd-8864-5a12ebc458b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4420b7-2d11-5d0b-af4b-bf52b0dda88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b7b194-3101-5717-a973-1aa6c440f7cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b203659-212c-5753-890d-57b8b9e5dd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322844} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8d721c-780d-5519-9582-b87d53c57a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322845} {:answer/id #uuid "be40c5ad-4683-5f9c-b462-7c98131f72c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322846} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ebd713-fd46-50cc-a358-f66b4b495bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322847} {:answer/id #uuid "e736087a-af37-5928-b3b6-f2fe59eb6150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb8d721c-780d-5519-9582-b87d53c57a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4915e457-0135-5dc0-abea-959ebd4f897c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abb67c02-615f-59af-8d9d-5f7f6c913b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36346" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(1,1)}{‖∇g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{i+j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef299fd-3808-54bd-9ffb-077cd2b50837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x-7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322851} {:answer/id #uuid "a78db14b-f527-5d32-9140-a7d7fd3a0a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+28=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322852} {:answer/id #uuid "c9297e72-1bc1-576b-a092-063e0274183a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322853} {:answer/id #uuid "20a6c3bd-a054-53bd-9821-f41e7a506721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y-139=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322854} {:answer/id #uuid "0916672c-7c4b-5a17-8048-77cbb5ef1202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0916672c-7c4b-5a17-8048-77cbb5ef1202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322855} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec70da3-a71a-537a-8116-95cc5283e8dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d72ff87-4a08-5245-b75d-f9edd1a5315d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36347" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0836dd-7f8d-5691-b976-cc7b692ee4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322858} {:answer/id #uuid "43e370c9-8adc-508b-ae81-dec446f6bf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322859} {:answer/id #uuid "863b837e-76b5-5969-8df3-62acae5a143c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4bf7de-a3b6-5196-baa3-a4db02fbd3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322861} {:answer/id #uuid "0104e92e-1a05-5108-acd9-de0e2116dea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "863b837e-76b5-5969-8df3-62acae5a143c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bdfb3d8-07a3-5325-9caf-d2bd2248ede2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a91a6f8-cc47-5279-bcce-31ae7f779527" :question/math-academy-id "q-65168" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c2ac5d-00c4-5b64-af2a-e38153ef1eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322865} {:answer/id #uuid "b2914b8f-3b1d-5733-b9d1-f96355fa5898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322866} {:answer/id #uuid "a76b5dfd-675c-5091-8bdc-5803cfeb4ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322867} {:answer/id #uuid "64f37d83-d4cf-5e04-9d05-325abb088252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322868} {:answer/id #uuid "e852efe3-72f8-5736-83dc-fc084f0e22a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e852efe3-72f8-5736-83dc-fc084f0e22a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869} :answer-field/id #uuid "a092e732-a318-5062-8c19-916e15592195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35cbb02b-fbbb-5866-807d-249aebeee809" :question/math-academy-id "q-71370" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }i+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a9b5524-57a1-50cf-819b-89fae0bdfe82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322872} {:answer/id #uuid "766a7df1-dceb-55f3-b31e-96dbf9a2de47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322873} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8fd65d-cc05-55fb-b7a6-febe1130e963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322874} {:answer/id #uuid "5f16aa08-0ffb-5576-a013-105e8815def8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322875} {:answer/id #uuid "64273e28-93b8-5d0c-b07b-4408adc98a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9b5524-57a1-50cf-819b-89fae0bdfe82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4663a9e-16c1-5c93-b572-852a5a36232e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a90575bd-9679-58ea-a060-e33293c63f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-71374" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2(\\frac{3}{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d89805-37e1-5010-ace2-e1d14950134c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322879} {:answer/id #uuid "6a14d68d-0419-5d63-a147-890caadd27f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322880} {:answer/id #uuid "c064d1d2-4af5-5177-8d42-dc0519437209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322881} {:answer/id #uuid "6d32b96f-3d87-509b-bdc5-aba4a32f34b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322882} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a792f-55d4-52de-a9ea-9179d7f9c8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c064d1d2-4af5-5177-8d42-dc0519437209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322881} :answer-field/id #uuid "7862f7d6-5bab-559c-805c-5d9bfd1fb8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d803c55-9de5-5306-b06d-dcf79c8f1239" :question/math-academy-id "q-71381" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b6406b-986a-5fa0-bda7-c5bf77f5124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322886} {:answer/id #uuid "71982151-0955-55d2-8dd6-21bff01e173e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322887} {:answer/id #uuid "c0497a00-5e77-52cf-8a43-fa9f6f8d08d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322888} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0f0ad2-f181-5207-8f0a-23526349efd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} {:answer/id #uuid "facad33b-6fa8-53c5-9b07-bcacded2a777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e0f0ad2-f181-5207-8f0a-23526349efd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aac0396-8461-53ef-95c9-6222dc99a8eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c79c3b6c-2eb8-5187-a6a7-dda99f1e32a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71385" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3c6c1d-ae7a-50f0-970e-12603e07e9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322893} {:answer/id #uuid "18d09da2-9bce-56c8-a1a4-55b504f62940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322894} {:answer/id #uuid "df01a1e5-2f92-5dbf-93b6-07ba00c5251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322895} {:answer/id #uuid "89dd655b-7b38-5bd8-81de-533157f67d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322896} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ee9e81-b6aa-5e6a-b52e-11cb81eca412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df01a1e5-2f92-5dbf-93b6-07ba00c5251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322895} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb18e819-5242-5710-986b-75ec6fb8fe7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e876b2a-993c-5d99-82f5-7108f1c93929" :question/math-academy-id "q-71393" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c51456b-4ebe-5939-a2e9-a4797979ed5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322900} {:answer/id #uuid "901ed514-b81b-5323-bd8a-77faae704126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322901} {:answer/id #uuid "eb35cd0a-8e36-5a1c-bdc6-f0183cdc32eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322902} {:answer/id #uuid "5f48019a-d890-5dd3-b0cf-06455b4bc8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322903} {:answer/id #uuid "593fe33d-84a0-5d89-8bc8-8032e7d8bf80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "593fe33d-84a0-5d89-8bc8-8032e7d8bf80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa0dc88-da73-54f0-92e7-bab1b2b5426c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284705cf-7f83-5939-969d-22a99241f89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1f799e-db9d-5c73-a4a1-224f1c576c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322908} {:answer/id #uuid "33c5cc2f-2427-5162-ac48-6203e88fbc02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322909} {:answer/id #uuid "86bf8ff9-336b-5fb4-b096-6bd1e8a049c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322910} {:answer/id #uuid "ae30582f-9357-5179-88b3-13d72c5a56b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f1f799e-db9d-5c73-a4a1-224f1c576c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322908} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5139e1-3e95-5995-bd4b-183d75f51bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a272103-726d-56b1-8c63-1b97e662e34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71403" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b661a14d-a933-5390-bb18-a029d3af2e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322914} {:answer/id #uuid "53936b14-19a2-5bfa-af0e-04c4b4cd09f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322915} {:answer/id #uuid "36587ced-e94f-583d-9084-b6d5de04bc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322916} {:answer/id #uuid "4603d4c4-f0dc-50db-b1e8-6e7f9d0b518d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322917} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a618f1-370f-58a0-9ad4-7625b95d0d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53936b14-19a2-5bfa-af0e-04c4b4cd09f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322915} :answer-field/id #uuid "562cd7b1-d727-50b2-a1ea-c88c6cae0079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "252876c9-7962-5371-8168-be2f8e38a47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71404" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e460517d-1510-58a0-8403-99223a713357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322921} {:answer/id #uuid "21f39c96-e887-5a55-bed0-44bf0a59ad64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322922} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1e4e09-ca95-572e-b993-7475841863f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322923} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fae3f4-e50e-5548-9c02-05ebc1598c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=z-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322924} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3c857-c4b3-5683-8199-1b81a7c465cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e460517d-1510-58a0-8403-99223a713357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322921} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fba1b21-948c-5932-a272-2a0adb408b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13503954-2456-5c13-9c01-1b77ab4bd258" :question/math-academy-id "q-71411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
